  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to "is physiotherapy a good career"</h2>

  <p>
    Physiotherapy is a real, regulated, growing clinical career in India, not a dressed-up fitness job with no
    ceiling. But "is physiotherapy good" and "will the first few years feel good on your body" are two different
    questions, and most course brochures blur them together until you cannot tell which one is actually being
    answered.
  </p>

  <p>
    The honest split is this: the daily reality — manual therapy, patient transfers, long stretches on your feet,
    and modest starting pay — is genuinely demanding and not exaggerated. The payoff on the other side —
    specialization pay, private-practice income, and a fast-growing home-healthcare segment — is also genuinely
    real, but it belongs to people who build a deliberate skill stack and treat the early grind as clinical
    skill-building, not a personal failure.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      This is not the "physiotherapy is a noble healing profession, join now" pitch flooding college admission
      material, and it is not a warning to avoid healthcare careers entirely either. Both miss the point. The field
      genuinely rewards people who go in with accurate expectations about the physical demand and build real
      specialization — and it genuinely wears down people who went in on the "helping people" idea alone, without
      testing whether their own body can sustain the work.
    </p>
    <p>
      One more thing this decision is not: a low-cost trial you can test through a YouTube course or a certificate
      site. Physiotherapy is a formally regulated clinical profession — the legal minimum is a BPT (or equivalent)
      degree from an NCAHP-recognised institution, a compulsory supervised internship, and registration with your
      State Physiotherapy Council before you can practise or use the title. There is no self-taught shortcut into
      this field, so the "is it good" question has to be answered before you commit four-plus years and real fees,
      not after.
    </p>
  </div>

