  <h2 id="own-injury">The injury data nobody puts in the brochure</h2>

  <p>
    This is the section most physiotherapy college websites skip entirely, because it does not make for a good
    admissions pitch. It is also one of the single most useful things to understand before you commit four-plus
    years and real fees to this path — because it is a fact about the profession, not about any one weak clinic or
    unlucky therapist.
  </p>

  <p>
    Across a global systematic review of occupational-health research, physiotherapists report work-related
    musculoskeletal disorders at a strikingly high frequency — roughly 75% overall, led by low back pain (around
    55%), followed by neck pain, upper back pain, shoulder strain, and hand or wrist problems. Separate research
    puts lifetime low-back-pain prevalence among physiotherapists as high as 80%. The documented causes are exactly
    what you would expect: bending and twisting to transfer patients, sustained awkward postures during manual
    therapy, and repeating the same physical movements dozens of times a day.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--gold">
    <p>
      None of this means avoid physiotherapy. It means go in aware that the profession which treats musculoskeletal
      injury in patients also causes it in a large share of its own practitioners — and build the posture, body
      mechanics, and workload-pacing habits from year one that reduce that risk, rather than learning them the hard
      way after your first injury.
    </p>
    <p>
      India-specific occupational-injury data for physiotherapists is still thin, so treat the figures above as
      global occupational-health signal rather than an India-specific statistic — but the physical mechanics behind
      manual therapy and patient transfers do not change by country.
    </p>
  </div>

